    "practice": "What is the purpose of a blameless postmortem?|Learn contributing conditions and improve systems|Avoid recording any human actions|Assign punishment from the first hypothesis|Replace all operational ownership|Blameless review preserves facts while focusing on learning and prevention.\nWhat makes an action item accountable?|A responsible owner and observable completion criterion|Only a task title because a named objective automatically assigns its owner and acceptance criteria|Only an incident nickname|Only a longer document|Action items need responsibility and a way to determine completion.\nIs a trigger always the complete explanation of an incident?|No|Always|Only for releases|Only for databases|Contributing conditions can shape why a trigger caused widespread harm.\nA report says operators should be more careful. What improvement is more useful?|A specific tested safeguard addressing the observed failure path|Require more personal caution without changing the tools or guardrails that shaped the failure path|Avoid documenting the failure because less evidence reduces the chance of another incident|A bigger list of vague reminders|Concrete controls can change system behavior and be verified.\nA timeline mixes guesses and confirmed observations. What should review require?|Clear evidence labels and source context|Removal of every uncertainty|Treating all guesses as proven facts|Only changing the document title|Evidence labeling keeps the causal narrative honest.\nThe fixture flags be careful as incomplete. What did it test?|Presence of ownership and acceptance fields|It validated the actual risk reduction and causal effectiveness of the proposed production safeguard|The organization\u2019s entire culture|The incident\u2019s true cause|Field checks cannot evaluate improvement effectiveness.",
